Correct the population edge: -0.43c was the old fill model, not the answer

Prompted by a direct question about whether the strategy is profitable, which
exposed an inconsistency in my own reporting.

I have been quoting -0.43c as the fill-corrected population edge and calling
the strategy "below zero". That figure used the OLD fill model (0.843/0.962),
estimated on 286 orders BEFORE the 17 late-closing outcomes were recovered -
the same survivorship-biased cohort this project has corrected twice already.
It was also computed per FILLED contract while the comparison number was per
SUBMITTED, so two different denominators were being set against each other.

  fill model                 E[maker|FILLED]   EV per SUBMITTED
  0.843/0.962   (old)             -0.43c            -0.37c
  0.8848/0.9884 (corrected)       +0.02c            +0.02c

On 8,187 in-band markets over 73 days, day-clustered, the corrected model
gives +0.02c per submitted contract, 95% CI [-1.57, +1.62], P(<=0) 0.4920.

The defensible claim is therefore INDISTINGUISHABLE FROM ZERO, not negative.
"Every band is below zero after fill correction" and "the strategy is past its
structural limit - it is below zero" both overstate the evidence. The raw edge
(+2.29c) is real and maker fill bias consumes essentially all of it; what
remains is unmeasurable on 73 days, in either direction.

This does not change the PTC verdict, which remains FAIL for its own reasons:
six commitments over two days, one order carrying 59% of the result, every
comparison interval spanning zero, and a 2s latency handicap that improves the
result 4.6x. It does change the framing of the base strategy PTC would replace.

SYNTHESIS.md and research/results/PTC_FINAL.md now carry the correction at the
top rather than silently edited figures, so the earlier claim and its
retraction are both visible.

